        "original_id": "b6ca64be-fd3d-59b4-9ff0-a494c31cbc15",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jacques Demy, Paul Sloane, Cary Grant, Joan Bennett, Randolph Scott, Walter Brennan, Stepin Fetchit\n- Works: The Umbrellas of Cherbourg, The Young Girls of Rochefort, Hearts in Dixie, The Woman Accused, The Texans, Break of Day\n- Roles: film directors, writer\n\nFacts:\n- Paul Sloane was born on April 19, 1893, and he became a writer and film director.\n- Paul Sloane directed 26 films from 1925 to 1952 and wrote or co-wrote 35 films.\n- Paul Sloane's films include Hearts in Dixie (1929), which featured Stepin Fetchit.\n- Paul Sloane directed The Woman Accused (1933), which featured Cary Grant.\n- Paul Sloane directed The Texans, which featured Joan Bennett, Randolph Scott, and Walter Brennan.\n- Jacques Demy is best known for the mid-1960s musicals The Umbrellas of Cherbourg (1964) and The Young Girls of Rochefort (1967).\n- Break of Day was adapted into a 1980 film directed by Jacques Demy.\n- Paul Sloane died on November 15, 1963.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
